import{_ as t,o as i,c as n,a2 as o}from"./chunks/framework.BXzK3EA4.js";const a="/assets/cec62239767828bf.cSNT0bVW.png",s="/assets/3fbbf14cb6c0dada.D9NPt0ke.png",l="/assets/b2afc1b626d94611.CXqWCanG.png",b=JSON.parse('{"title":"How to enable/disable ping on Linux system","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"How to enable/disable ping on Linux system"},"headers":[],"relativePath":"operations-knowledge-base/operating-system/linux/how-to-enable-disable-ping-on-linux-system.md","filePath":"operations-knowledge-base/operating-system/linux/how-to-enable-disable-ping-on-linux-system.md","lastUpdated":1794311093000}'),p={name:"operations-knowledge-base/operating-system/linux/how-to-enable-disable-ping-on-linux-system.md"};function c(r,e,h,d,g,m){return i(),n("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[o('<p>To prevent others from discovering and potentially attacking your machine through network ping scans, you can configure your system to disable or block the ping command.</p><p>Linux systems allow ping responses by default. The ability to ping is determined by two factors:</p><ul><li>Kernel parameters</li><li>Firewall</li></ul><p>To allow ping, both factors need to be enabled. If either of the two factors prohibits ping, it will not be possible to ping the system.</p><ol><li>The method to disable ping at the kernel level is as follows:</li></ol><p>First, let&#39;s check if the IP is responding properly by using the ping.pe website. Input the IP and test the connectivity. This will show if the target IP allows ping requests.</p><p><img src="'+a+'" alt=""></p><p>Temporarily disabling ping. ：</p><h2 id="echo-1-proc-sys-net-ipv4-icmp-echo-ignore-all" tabindex="-1">echo 1 &gt;/proc/sys/net/ipv4/ icmp_echo_ignore_all <a class="header-anchor" href="#echo-1-proc-sys-net-ipv4-icmp-echo-ignore-all" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;echo 1 &gt;/proc/sys/net/ipv4/ icmp\\_echo\\_ignore\\_all&quot;">​</a></h2><p>At this point, if we check the IP, we can see that ping is disabled. If we still want to test the IP connectivity, we can perform a ping test on a specific port. Here, we can see that the connection is successful. Since ping is temporarily disabled, the ping functionality will be restored after a machine reboot.</p><p><img src="'+s+'" alt=""><img src="'+l+'" alt=""></p><p>Permanent disabling of ping</p><p>#vi /etc/sysctl.conf #Enter the configuration file.</p><p>Add a line inside：net.ipv4.icmp _echo_ignore_all= 1</p><p>If you already have &quot;net.ipv4.icmp_echo_ignore_all&quot; in the file, you can directly modify the value after the &quot;=&quot; sign. (0 means allow, 1 means deny)</p><h2 id="sysctl-p-apply-the-new-configuration" tabindex="-1">sysctl -p #Apply the new configuration. <a class="header-anchor" href="#sysctl-p-apply-the-new-configuration" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;sysctl -p    #Apply the new configuration.&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Check if ping is disabled.</p><h2 id="cat-proc-sys-net-ipv4-icmp-echo-ignore-all" tabindex="-1">cat /proc/sys/net/ipv4/icmp_echo_ignore_all <a class="header-anchor" href="#cat-proc-sys-net-ipv4-icmp-echo-ignore-all" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;cat /proc/sys/net/ipv4/icmp\\_echo\\_ignore\\_all&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The value &quot;1&quot; indicates that ping is disabled, while the value &quot;0&quot; indicates that ping is enabled.</p>',19)])])}const y=t(p,[["render",c]]);export{b as __pageData,y as default};
